<section class="wx-cpt__strip" aria-label="What drives front-of-house cleaning cost"><div class="wx-cpt__wrap"><div class="wx-cpt__strip-grid"><div class="wx-cpt__strip-cell"><strong>Q4 volume</strong><span>Parties, holiday bookings and private events multiply every reset</span></div><div class="wx-cpt__strip-cell"><strong>Three turns an hour</strong><span>Every extra trip costs seat time at your busiest</span></div><div class="wx-cpt__strip-cell"><strong>Labour 30-35% of expenses</strong><span>The loaded rate is what makes the minute expensive</span></div><div class="wx-cpt__strip-cell"><strong>Par levels set now</strong><span>Year-end is when supply spend gets reviewed</span></div></div></div></section>
<article class="wx-cpt__article">
<section class="wx-cpt__sec">
<div class="wx-cpt__read">
<p>That is why cost per wipe is the wrong unit to manage. Cost per wipe tells you what you spent. <strong>Cost per table</strong> tells you what the seat cost you — and that is the number your P&amp;L is actually denominated in.</p>
<p>Table Bussers® pre-mixed table wipes make that arithmetic simple: there is one consumable to count, and it is already at the table when the guest stands. By the end of this guide you will be able to calculate your own cost per table, see where the minutes disappear during a reset, and reduce both without lowering the standard a guest notices.</p>
</div>

</section>
<section class="wx-cpt__sec" id="quick-answer--key-takeaways-{{ uid }}">
<div class="wx-cpt__read">
<h2 id="quick-answer--key-takeaways-{{ uid }}">Quick answer / key takeaways</h2>
<ul class="wx-cpt__takeaways"><li>A pre-mixed table wipe works out at roughly <strong>$0.09</strong>, so a two-wipe reset costs about <strong>$0.15–$0.18 per table</strong> in consumables.</li><li>The reset target most operators work to is <strong>under 90 seconds</strong> (Lavu, checked 23 Sep 2026).</li><li>Casual dining averages about <strong>1.5 turns per hour</strong> (Worldmetrics, 2026).</li><li>Labour is <strong>30–35% of expenses</strong> in casual dining — the biggest single lever on what a reset really costs (Worldmetrics, 2026).</li><li><strong>Cost per table is not cost per wipe.</strong> One is the unit you buy in; the other is the unit you are paid in.</li></ul>
</div>
<div class="wx-cpt__stats-block"><div class="wx-cpt__read"><div class="wx-cpt__stats"><div class="wx-cpt__stat"><b>$0.09</b><span>consumable per table, one wipe per reset</span></div><div class="wx-cpt__stat"><b>$0.29</b><span>cost per table in a 20-table room</span></div><div class="wx-cpt__stat"><b>90 seconds</b><span>target reset time most operators work to</span></div><div class="wx-cpt__stat"><b>1.5 turns/hour</b><span>casual-dining average (Worldmetrics, 2026)</span></div></div></div></div>
</section>
