<section class="wx-cpt__sec" id="what-cost-per-table-means-{{ uid }}">
<div class="wx-cpt__read">
<h2 id="what-cost-per-table-means-{{ uid }}">What cost per table means</h2>
<p>Cost per table is the total cost of returning one table to service — the consumable, the labour minutes, and the share of supplies a single reset consumes — divided by how many times the table turns.</p>
<p>It is deliberately not the same as cost per wipe. Cost per wipe is a purchasing figure: it tells you which format is cheaper to buy. Cost per table is an operating figure: it tells you which setup is cheaper to <em>run</em>, including the seconds your staff spend on it. Operators who manage only the first number routinely pay more for the second.</p>
<p>Here is the difference in one line. A cheaper wipe that takes two trips per table is more expensive than a slightly dearer one that takes a single pass.</p>
<h3 id="the-three-numbers-you-are-really-adding-up-{{ uid }}">The three numbers you are really adding up</h3>
<div class="wx-cpt__tablewrap" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Comparison table">
<table class="wx-cpt__table"><thead><tr>
<th scope="col">Component</th>
<th scope="col">What goes in</th>
<th scope="col">Where it comes from</th>
</tr></thead><tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row" data-label="Component">Consumable</th>
<td data-label="What goes in">the wipe, at your case rate</td>
<td data-label="Where it comes from">your supplier invoice</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row" data-label="Component">Reset labour</th>
<td data-label="What goes in">minutes per reset × loaded hourly rate</td>
<td data-label="Where it comes from">your schedule and payroll</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row" data-label="Component">Reset count</th>
<td data-label="What goes in">turns per service × services per week</td>
<td data-label="Where it comes from">your POS and your floor</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The first is the only one most purchasing decisions look at. It is also the smallest.</p>
</div>

</section>
<section class="wx-cpt__sec" id="why-q4-is-the-moment-to-measure-it-{{ uid }}">
<div class="wx-cpt__read">
<h2 id="why-q4-is-the-moment-to-measure-it-{{ uid }}">Why Q4 is the moment to measure it</h2>
<p>Year-end is when you set next year's par levels and when your supply spend gets reviewed. It is also when front-of-house volume climbs: parties, holiday bookings, private events.</p>
<p>Higher volume does not create a cleaning problem — it multiplies whatever reset routine you already have. If a reset takes three minutes, a busier dining room turns that into three minutes of lost seat time more often, not less often.</p>
<p>That is the argument for measuring cost per table now: the number you calculate in a quiet week is the number you will be running at your busiest, only more times over.</p>
</div>

</section>
<section class="wx-cpt__sec" id="the-four-step-reset-{{ uid }}">
<div class="wx-cpt__read">
<h2 id="the-four-step-reset-{{ uid }}">The four-step reset</h2>
<p>Every reset, in every operation, is the same four moves:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Clear</strong> — plates, glassware and linen leave the table.</li><li><strong>Clean</strong> — the surface is wiped, in one pass, before anything is set back down.</li><li><strong>Reset</strong> — cutlery, glassware, condiments and settings return.</li><li><strong>Seat</strong> — the host is told the table is ready.</li></ol>
<p>We covered this workflow in detail in <a href="https://wipex.co/blogs/library/table-busser-restaurant-efficiency">how table bussers improve restaurant efficiency and table turnover</a> — read that for the staffing and training side. This article stays on the money: what each step costs, and how to bring the cleaning step down without slowing the room.</p>
<p>The cleaning step is the one operators under-measure, because it looks like a small action repeated many times. It is also the step where supplies and minutes meet.</p>
</div>
