<section class="wx-cpt__sec" id="how-to-calculate-your-own-cost-per-table-{{ uid }}">
<div class="wx-cpt__read">
<h2 id="how-to-calculate-your-own-cost-per-table-{{ uid }}">How to calculate your own cost per table</h2>
<p>Four inputs, one multiplication.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1 — Your cost per wipe.</strong> Take the case price and divide by the wipe count. At the <a href="https://wipex.co/products/table-bussers-surface-wipes">400-count Table Bussers® bucket</a> price that is roughly <strong>$0.09 per wipe</strong>; buy the four-pack case and it is about <strong>$0.075 per wipe</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2 — Wipes per reset.</strong> One wipe per table in a single pass is the target. Allow two if your team commonly returns for a second wipe, and count that second trip in Step 4 as well.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3 — Consumable cost per table.</strong> <code>wipes per reset × cost per wipe</code>. At one wipe, about $0.09. At two, about $0.18.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4 — Add the labour minute.</strong> Multiply minutes per reset by your loaded hourly rate and divide by 60. At a $16 loaded rate, 45 seconds of reset labour is about <strong>$0.12</strong>.</p>
<pre class="wx-cpt__code"><code>cost per table = (wipes per reset × cost per wipe) + (reset minutes ÷ 60 × loaded hourly rate)</code></pre>
<p>Run it with real numbers and the picture changes. <strong>Labour is 30–35% of expenses in casual dining</strong> (Worldmetrics, 2026), so it is the term that dominates the equation — and the term a one-pass wipe actually moves.</p>
<div class="wx-cpt__calc" data-wpx-calc data-module="cost-per-use" id="wx-cpt-calc-{{ uid }}"><h3 class="wx-cpt__calc-h">Calculate your cost per table</h3><div class="wx-cpt__calc-grid"><label>Tables<input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="1" min="0" value="20" data-k="tables"></label><label>Turns per service<input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="0.5" min="0" value="3" data-k="turns"></label><label>Services per week<input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="1" min="0" value="6" data-k="services"></label><label>Case price ($)<input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="0.01" min="0" value="36.99" data-k="price"></label><label>Wipes per case<input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="1" min="1" value="400" data-k="count"></label><label>Wipes per reset<input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="1" min="1" value="2" data-k="wipes"></label><label>Seconds per reset<input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="5" min="0" value="45" data-k="seconds"></label><label>Loaded hourly rate ($)<input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="0.5" min="0" value="16" data-k="rate"></label></div><div class="wx-cpt__calc-out" role="status" aria-live="polite"><div><span data-out="table">&mdash;</span><small>cost per table</small></div><div><span data-out="week">&mdash;</span><small>weekly cleaning cost</small></div><div><span data-out="cover">&mdash;</span><small>cost per cover</small></div></div><p class="wx-cpt__calc-note">Cost figures only, based on the prices you enter. Check your own invoice.</p></div>
<h3 id="worked-example-a-20-table-room-{{ uid }}">Worked example: a 20-table room</h3>
<p>A 20-table dining room, three turns per table per evening, six services a week:</p>
<div class="wx-cpt__tablewrap" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Comparison table">
<table class="wx-cpt__table"><thead><tr>
<th scope="col">Line</th>
<th scope="col">Assumption</th>
<th scope="col">Weekly</th>
</tr></thead><tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row" data-label="Line">Resets</th>
<td data-label="Assumption">20 tables × 3 turns × 6 services</td>
<td data-label="Weekly">360 resets</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row" data-label="Line">Consumable</th>
<td data-label="Assumption">360 × $0.09</td>
<td data-label="Weekly">$32.40</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row" data-label="Line">Reset labour</th>
<td data-label="Assumption">360 × 45 sec = 4.5 hours × $16</td>
<td data-label="Weekly">$72.00</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row" data-label="Line"><strong>Total</strong></th>
<td data-label="Assumption"></td>
<td data-label="Weekly"><strong>$104.40</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row" data-label="Line"><strong>Per table</strong></th>
<td data-label="Assumption">$104.40 ÷ 360</td>
<td data-label="Weekly"><strong>$0.29</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Consumables are under a third of the real cost. The rest is the minute.</p>
</div>
<div class="wx-cpt__sec-block wx-cpt__system"><span class="wx-cpt__eyebrow">The system</span><h2>What a pre-mixed wipe actually changes</h2><div class="wx-cpt__system-eq"><span class="wx-cpt__pill">400-count bucket at the station</span><span class="wx-cpt__op" aria-hidden="true">+</span><span class="wx-cpt__pill">one wipe, one pass</span><span class="wx-cpt__op" aria-hidden="true">=</span><span class="wx-cpt__pill wx-cpt__system-result">a reset that never needs a second trip</span></div><p>It removes the fetch, not the standard: the wipe is already where the cleaning happens, so the cleaning step starts at the table.</p><p class="wx-cpt__cta-row" style="justify-content:center;"><a class="wx-cpt__btn wx-cpt__btn--primary" data-cro="@CRO@-system" href="https://wipex.co/products/table-bussers-surface-wipes">Shop Table Bussers® →</a></p></div>
</section>
<section class="wx-cpt__sec" id="what-your-current-setup-actually-costs-{{ uid }}">
<div class="wx-cpt__read">
<h2 id="what-your-current-setup-actually-costs-{{ uid }}">What your current setup actually costs</h2>
<p>If you are running paper towels and a spray, the consumable looks cheap per sheet — around <strong>5¢ a sheet</strong> (The Zero Store, 2026). The reset, though, usually involves a spray bottle and a second trip. It also leaves a wet surface that has to dry before you can set the table.</p>
<p>Laundry is the other invisible line. Operators spend <strong>upwards of $200,000 a year</strong> getting linen clean (Berkwiper, distributor guide) — and a large part of that volume is table linen that a pre-mixed wipe routine replaces entirely.</p>
<p>None of this argues that one approach is cheaper than another. It argues for measuring both on the same basis: per table, per reset, including the minute.</p>
<h3 id="what-a-one-pass-cleaning-step-changes-{{ uid }}">What a one-pass cleaning step changes</h3>
<p>A dedicated busser can cut reset time by roughly half (Restaurant Booking System, Academy). The cleaning step sits inside that time. If the wipe is already at the table, mixed and ready, the busser never leaves the station to fetch a spray or a cloth.</p>
<p>That is the whole proposition of a pre-mixed table wipe: it removes a trip, not a standard.</p>
</div>
<div class="wx-cpt__sec-block"><div class="wx-cpt__feature"><div class="wx-cpt__feature-body"><span class="wx-cpt__eyebrow">The workhorse</span><h2>One consumable, already at the table</h2><p>One bucket is the whole purchase: no spray, no cloth, and no walk to the station during a reset.</p><ul class="wx-cpt__feature-list"><li>Removes the fetch from the cleaning step</li><li>One wipe per table in a single pass</li><li>Keeps the standard a guest notices</li></ul><a class="wx-cpt__btn wx-cpt__btn--primary" data-cro="@CRO@-feature" href="https://wipex.co/products/table-bussers-surface-wipes">Shop Table Bussers® →</a></div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="wx-cpt__sec" id="the-five-places-minutes-hide-in-a-reset-{{ uid }}">
<div class="wx-cpt__read">
<h2 id="the-five-places-minutes-hide-in-a-reset-{{ uid }}">The five places minutes hide in a reset</h2>
<p>Measure one reset with a stopwatch and you will usually find the time is not in the wiping. It is in these five places:</p>
<ol><li><strong>The fetch.</strong> The busser leaves the table to find the spray, the cloth or the wipe. This is usually the single largest cost, and it exists only because the supplies are not at the station.</li></ol>
<ol><li><strong>The second pass.</strong> The first wipe left residue or streaks, so it is done again. One pass is the target; two is a routine that needs changing.</li><li><strong>The wet wait.</strong> A wet surface cannot be set. Tableware placed on a damp top gets re-cleared when the guest sits.</li><li><strong>The handoff.</strong> The table is ready but nobody has told the host, so it sits empty in a queue.</li><li><strong>The Monday-morning redo.</strong> Areas missed at peak get cleaned again at close, at overtime rates.</li></ol>
<p>Only one of those five is the actual cleaning. The other four are layout, stationing and communication problems wearing a cleaning costume.</p>
<p>Fix the first one — put the bucket at the station — and you fix the second and third most of the time, because the busser is no longer walking and no longer rushing.</p>
</div>

</section>
<section class="wx-cpt__sec" id="bar-patio-and-private-room-three-rooms-three-costs-{{ uid }}">
<div class="wx-cpt__read">
<h2 id="bar-patio-and-private-room-three-rooms-three-costs-{{ uid }}">Bar, patio and private room: three rooms, three costs</h2>
<p>Cost per table is not one number for a whole venue. Three rooms usually carry three different resets, and they should be measured separately.</p>
<h3 id="dining-room-{{ uid }}">Dining room</h3>
<p>The dining room is the baseline: seated covers, a full reset, the highest labour share per table.</p>
<h3 id="bar-{{ uid }}">Bar</h3>
<p>The bar resets more often and more shallowly. Bar tops are deep-cleaned less frequently, so the consumable count per reset is lower, but the reset count per hour is higher — which pushes the labour term up even when the consumable term falls.</p>
<h3 id="patio-and-private-room-{{ uid }}">Patio and private room</h3>
<p>Patios and private rooms are seasonal. A patio that opens for six months a year has its own par level and its own scent decision, because the room may be partly open to the street.</p>
<p>Two formats help here rather than a single SKU across the venue: <a href="https://wipex.co/products/table-bussers-surface-wipes">Table Bussers® Autumn-Scented</a> for the guest-facing rooms and <a href="https://wipex.co/products/table-bussers-unscented">Unscented</a> where guests should not notice anything at all. The certification and food-service documentation belongs to the Autumn SKU only — see the comparison below.</p>
</div>

</section>
<section class="wx-cpt__sec" id="setting-a-par-level-from-your-reset-count-{{ uid }}">
<div class="wx-cpt__read">
<h2 id="setting-a-par-level-from-your-reset-count-{{ uid }}">Setting a par level from your reset count</h2>
<p>Most operators set a par level from instinct, then discover it in the middle of a Friday. The arithmetic is five minutes of work.</p>
<ol><li>Count resets per service on a normal week: tables × turns.</li><li>Multiply by wipes per reset.</li><li>Multiply by services per week, then multiply by the weeks between deliveries.</li><li>Add a buffer worth your busiest two weeks of the year — for most venues, that is December.</li></ol>
<p>Run it: 20 tables × 3 turns × 6 services = 360 resets a week. At one wipe per reset that is 360 wipes a week, or just under one 400-count bucket. Two buckets covers a week with the buffer already in it.</p>
<p>The point of a par level is not to hold more stock. It is to make the fetch unnecessary, because there is always a bucket within reach of the station.</p>
</div>

</section>
