<section class="wx-cpt__sec" id="self-assessment-your-cost-per-table-and-reset-time-{{ uid }}">
<div class="wx-cpt__liferow">
<div class="wx-cpt__read">
<h2 id="self-assessment-your-cost-per-table-and-reset-time-{{ uid }}">Self-assessment: your cost per table and reset time</h2>
<p>Run this on one section of your floor. It takes one service to fill in.</p>
<p><strong>Reset time</strong></p>
<ol><li>Time three resets with a phone stopwatch. Start when the guest stands, stop when the table is set.</li><li>Average them.</li><li>If the average is above 90 seconds, watch one reset and count the trips your busser makes. Trips, not effort, are usually the delay.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Cost per table</strong></p>
<ol><li>Case price ÷ wipe count = cost per wipe.</li><li>× wipes per reset = consumable per table.</li><li>× minutes ÷ 60 × loaded hourly rate = labour per table.</li><li>Add the two.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Then compare against the room</strong></p>
<ul><li>Weekly resets = tables × turns × services.</li><li>Weekly cleaning cost = weekly resets × cost per table.</li><li>Cost per cover = weekly cleaning cost ÷ covers served.</li></ul>
<p>If cost per cover is a number you have never seen written down, you are not alone — most operators can quote their food cost to one decimal place and have never calculated this one.</p>
</div>
